course: dictionary
lesson: 13956
title: Two meanings of one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A lightbulb is a glass or plastic bulb that gives out light when electricity goes through it. You can see one in a lamp or on a ceiling. «The lightbulb in the hall has gone out, so we need a new one.» The words gone out and new one tell you it is the thing that makes light.
explain: Some words have a second, informal meaning. «Suddenly I had a lightbulb moment and saw how the puzzle worked.» Nothing is lit up here. A lightbulb moment is a sudden moment when you understand something or have a great idea. Ask yourself: does the meaning fit the sentence? If not, try the other meaning.
explain: Look for clues that show which meaning the writer wants. A lamp, a switch or electricity points to the real bulb. A puzzle, a question or a sudden understanding points to the idea. The context decides.
fact: A lightbulb gives out light when electricity goes through it.
fact: «Lightbulb moment» is an informal phrase for a sudden idea or understanding.
fact: In cartoons, a lightbulb over someone's head shows that they have had an idea.
fact: Thomas Edison and Joseph Swan both worked on early electric lightbulbs.
fact: Let a bulb cool before you touch it, and switch it off before you change it.
exercise: «The lightbulb in the kitchen has gone out.» What is a lightbulb here? | A kind of cooking pot | *A glass or plastic bulb that gives out light | A small wooden table
exercise: «I had a lightbulb moment and finally saw how the sum worked.» What does lightbulb moment mean? | A moment when the power goes off | A moment when a bulb breaks | A moment of silence | *A sudden moment when you understand something
exercise: In a cartoon, a lightbulb appears over a character's head. What does it show? | The character is turning a lamp on | The character has switched off the power | The character is cold | *The character has an idea
test: Why should you let a lightbulb cool before touching it? | Because bulbs get heavier when they are hot | *Because it could be hot enough to burn you | Because cool bulbs give out more light
test: Which sentence uses lightbulb with its informal meaning? | She put the lightbulb in the box | The lamp by my bed needs a new lightbulb | The lightbulb was made of glass | *When I read the clue, I had a lightbulb moment
exam: «After an hour staring at the puzzle, Lily had a lightbulb moment and laughed.» What does lightbulb moment mean here? | A phone call from school | *A sudden idea or understanding | A loud bang from a broken lamp | A walk in the dark
